From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Morinaga <me@gustavomorinaga.dev>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 20:48:48 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Vulkan: the host overlay is blended over the window, not
 copied

Patch 2097's overlay copied its pixels over the window's image as they were, so a port's notice was opaque: Project
Vekta's trophy card, drawn at 90-94 % alpha in its design, hid the frame under it, and its cut corners had to be left
out as rows of rects.

Fix: the overlay's pixels carry premultiplied alpha in their fourth byte and are blended, colour + frame * (1 - alpha).
The pass works on buffers, so the swap chain still needs no colour-attachment or storage use, no render pass and no
graphics pipeline: after the blit the pixels under the rects are copied out of the swap chain image (in GENERAL
layout) into a second host buffer in the overlay's own layout, one compute dispatch (vulkan/rsx_vk_overlay.comp, the
byte order the window's, only the fourth byte alpha) blends them there, and they are copied back; the image goes back
to TRANSFER_DST_OPTIMAL for what follows. The swap chain asks for TRANSFER_SRC whenever the surface allows it (it was
asked only for PS3RECOMP_VK_DUMP_PRESENT); without it, or if the pass cannot be made, the pixels are copied as before.

ENHANCEMENT: the PS3 game has no such overlay.
